[04:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'battery and how Pela actually solves the four problems we just walked through. So, how does Pela solve [music] these four huge problems with home batteries? Well, it starts with a total reorientation of how home batteries work'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and how they even work together. Pela calls their product a battery mesh operating system. Think mesh Wi-Fi, but for your home\'s energy. Remember when home Wi-Fi was one big router in your closet and half your house had no'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'signal? Well, mesh Wi-Fi fixed that with smaller units that talk to each other and coordinate. [music] The old home battery is that single router. This new category is mesh. A single Pela plugs into a standard'

[05:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '120-V outlet. No electrician. 1.6 kWh of storage. 2,400 W of continuous power. Plus a 3,600 W surge for up to 30 seconds and a 90-A instant peak [music] to kick on motor loads like a fridge'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compressor. And the units coordinate over a local network, so they keep talking even if your Wi-Fi drops. It\'s a battery buddy system. Put one in an apartment and that could be your whole system. Put up to 64 in a'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'house and they all coordinate as one. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; The team is already testing meshes of over a thousand units for whole apartment buildings. Start small, scale up and you can take them with you when you move. Combined, you\'ve got something more than a battery. You\'ve got the'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'potential for an entire energy hub. But, how it actually pulls this off is worth its own section, so I\'ll come back to that in just a minute. Now, here\'s the part that most reviews might gloss over. Apella hardware is'

[05:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'capable of feeding power back into your home through a standard outlet. Push and pull. That\'s how you would get true solar arbitrage and broader whole home energy distribution. That\'s how you would shave peak load during the day. That\'s the dream feature of this whole'

[06:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'category. Being able to power other devices in your home without having them plugged directly into the Apella. And right now, in the US at least, that\'s illegal. It\'s not the code. The rules just haven\'t caught up. Our electrical'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'code was written before a product like this even existed. [music] Plug-in solar, plug-in storage, bidirectional outlets, none of it fits the current rulebook. But, the wall is starting to crack. 28 states plus DC are actively considering plug-in solar and storage'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'legislation. Utah was the first to pass a balcony solar bill back in 2025. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; UL 3700, which is the safety standard this whole category needs, is actively being developed. And to be clear,'

[06:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Germany figured this out years ago. They\'re just ahead of us. And one more thing worth noting, you don\'t have to wait for the code to change to help the grid. A lot of utilities already pay homeowners for demand response. Normally, that means your AC gets cut'

[06:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'off in the hottest days of the year. With Apella, your AC could just shift to stored power instead. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; You stay cool and get paid. When the rules catch up, the ability to push power back into the wall outlet will roll out over time as utility pilots and'

[07:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'codes allow. But, here\'s my mantra. Buy the product for what it can do today, not for the promise of what it can do tomorrow. This is a regulatory wall, not a bug in the product. And the window to climb over it is almost open.'

[07:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Right now, Apella [music] is super useful for backup and load shifting, and it\'ll get even more useful over time. So, here\'s what it can do for you today. Each Apella has Wi-Fi and a cellular'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connection built in. Apella built this with a local first mindset, and that\'s the part I absolutely love. The units talk to each other over a local thread network, the same protocol a lot of smart home gear uses. If your Wi-Fi'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'drops or you live in a cellular dead zone, the batteries keep doing their job. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; For the smart home nerds like me, it also means that you can pull every Pillo into Home Assistant locally. Full sensor data, outlet level control, no internet'

[07:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'required. You get the benefit of Pillo\'s app and software stack, but you stay in control of the hardware for the long haul. That\'s rare in this category, and it\'s a huge deal. Now, Pillo pulls data from four places. Your rooftop solar,'

[08:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'currently Tesla and Enphase, but it can also use time and weather forecasting for solar coordination. Your utility, using smart regional defaults. Your appliances, because it meters every plug and learns each one\'s normal rhythm. And'

[08:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your smart home, through Home Assistant today, and Alexa and Google Home coming this summer. I\'ll show you some real-world use cases in just a minute. You can put the whole mesh, however many batteries that you have, into one of three modes. The first up is backup'

[08:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only. It\'s basic, but useful. The [music] battery stays fully charged and only discharges when the power goes out. While it waits, grid power passes straight through, so nothing gets wasted converting AC to DC and back. The second'

[08:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mode is save me money. This is for homes with time-of-use rates. The battery charges when electricity is cheap and discharges when it\'s expensive. Now, we\'re getting somewhere. And finally, there\'s the maximize my solar'

[09:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mode. This is the one that I\'m currently running. It charges the battery with solar during the day, then discharges when the sun goes down. You can also set a per battery reserve, so each unit always holds onto a guaranteed chunk for backup. And there\'s'

[09:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a storm mode, which I\'ve loved on my Powerwall and Enphase setups. If a big storm is forecast, the unit flips into backup mode automatically for energy security. You don\'t have to think about it, unless you really want to, like me. The hardware is designed to look like'

[09:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something you\'d actually want in your house. It\'s sleek, it\'s not utilitarian, it\'s more nice piece of home gear than portable [music] job site battery. Again, apologies to EcoFlow. I\'m sure you\'re lovely. Every Pela has a'

[09:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'touchscreen on the front and it does more than what most product displays do. Set up, Wi-Fi, home assistant pairing, outlet control, mode settings, all right there on the device. There\'s a live readout of your solar production and home energy'

[09:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use. An energy independence screen that shows how much of your power traces back to your own solar. If you just want to use it as a clock, you can do that, too. There\'s no mobile app required for any of that, which I really appreciate. Now,'

[10:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the mobile app is where the data lives. It\'s nice charts and graphs, energy usage per Pela and the whole mesh viewed as one. Also, runtime estimates. It\'s the dashboard view of everything the units'

[10:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are doing. This is every obsessive energy dad\'s dream made real. It\'s really kind of awesome. Okay, so what happens when you put three of these things into a house? And why would someone who already has a whole'

[10:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'home battery even do this? I\'m not just some Matty who is batty for batteries. I\'m just trying to get the most out of what I spent. When I built my home, I wanted 25 to 30 kWh of storage in the garage. I got stuck at 20. Any more capacity on the wall in'

[10:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'coding my area would have forced me to install a sprinkler system in the garage to keep going. That would have cost [music] thousands of dollars that home batteries are supposed to save. So, I had a gap, about 5 kWh I couldn\'t add into a house where I\'d already spent'

[10:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'serious money on home storage. Pela let me make up the difference by putting three batteries in three different rooms. So, let\'s take a little tour about how this actually works. I\'ve got a red Pela standing vertically next to my desk where I\'ve got a Mac Studio'

[11:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'plugged in along with desktop speakers and a subwoofer. There\'s also a floor lamp and a desk lamp. Everything that runs a work day. Now, while I was writing this very script, my office was pulling somewhere [music] between 80 and 150 W. The average is about 96. This'

[11:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gives me roughly 11 hours and 30 minutes of backup time from this single Pela, which is more than enough to ride through almost any outage I\'d ever see. And because the desk runs off my rooftop'

[11:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solar through Pila, my energy independence on that readout always reads 100%. But for me, the party trick is the auto shutoff. The Pila is hooked into my smart home, so when I leave the office and the room is empty, it'

[11:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'automatically powers down anything I\'ve told it to. The back outlet running my speaker system goes dark, so there\'s no phantom draw while I\'m not in the room. I don\'t have to think about it. The [music] room just gets quieter and a little more efficient on its own. And'

[11:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'during the work day, the same unit is my backup. The desktop and monitors run off the Pila instead of straight from the wall. If the power goes out mid-video render, which is the greatest fear of every YouTuber, I don\'t even notice. The switchover takes 20 milliseconds. It\'s'

[12:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so fast, I can\'t even notice it, and neither can my gear. My second unit is in my network closet. This is the one that real nerds out there will be most excited about. My network closet runs 300 W 24 hours a day, 365 days a year.'

[12:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And yes, that\'s a lot, but after all, I do run a business out of my home. That includes all the servers, switches, Wi-Fi access points in my house, security cameras,'

[12:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the whole Undecided with Matt Ferrell home and media tech stack is in there. While I have a UPS battery on this setup, it will only run the rack for about 10 minutes or so before it kicks out. It\'s just enough time to get the'

[12:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'computer gear safely turned off. I plugged in a Pila in line between the wall and the rack because this is one of the biggest energy sinks in my house. The Pila is helping to offload energy use from my Enphase battery system,'

[12:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which means there\'s more power available for things like my HVAC system. And just like my office setup, when the power blips, the rack doesn\'t even flicker. No reboot, no lost work, no fighting with a firewall after a brownout. And I get 5'

[13:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hours of backup instead of 10 minutes from my lead-acid battery UPS. The big win is shifting this load off the Enphase batteries to extend that usage elsewhere in the house. This means more of my solar generation is getting'

[13:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stored and used locally. The third unit goes where everyone else in the house goes in the evening, my living room. There\'s a lot of energy spent in this room, and I\'m not just talking about arguing over what to watch. The TV is always on and the home theater receiver'

[13:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is running along with lamps while my family sits down for a movie or show. That load runs for 3 to 4 hours straight every night. The Pika here does two things. During normal hours, it runs the home theater off of stored energy from my solar, which again helps [music] to'

[13:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'extend my Enphase battery system. During an outage, keeps the show going long enough that an outage doesn\'t turn into a family emergency or worse, an insurrection. And like in my home office, I use my smart home to switch off the living room'

[14:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'components to avoid phantom power drain. So, what do all these indoor batteries add up to? With three units in three rooms, that\'s roughly 4.8 kWh of additional storage. That is very close to the 5 kWh I couldn\'t add to the'

[14:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'garage because of that sprinkler rule. The gap I couldn\'t close with Enphase, I closed with three plug-in batteries in rooms that needed the most. And the Enphase system in the garage is a little happier for it. Or at least I think it\'s happy. It\'s more of a hummer than a'

[14:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'talker. The loads I moved off the main system at night don\'t need to get pulled from the garage batteries anymore. That makes more reserve for the rest of the house. I\'ve talked about this in my previous videos about my home\'s energy use, but I\'m net zero across the course of a'

[14:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'year. Meaning my solar panels produce as much power as I use. Day to day, that\'s a different story. How much I pull from my solar and battery setup versus the grid changes with the season. So, I ran the numbers before and after the Pika'

[14:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mesh went in. On a typical night before Pika, my Enphase system used to pull a few kWh from the grid in the early morning hours just to bridge the pre-dawn gap. After Pika? It\'s basically nothing. That 4.8 kWh of Pika storage is doing exactly what I had hoped. It'

[15:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'covers the shortfall that used to push the Enphase batteries to the reserve. I\'m making it through the full 24 hours on my own solar far more than I used to. Okay, so it\'s time to put my honest hat'

[15:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on because I said it would. A Pila is not a whole home backup. One unit at 1.6 kWh or a three-pack at 4.8 is not going to run your whole house during a multi-day outage. An expansion accessory'

[15:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shipping later this year doubles each unit 3.2 kWh, which raises the ceiling, but it still isn\'t enough to be a whole home backup. And at the moment, [music] the only things backed up have to be plugged directly into the Pila. If what'

[15:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you need is a truly whole home solution, you need Enphase or Powerwall. And if you\'ve got an EV, Pila can\'t handle it. A Pila is not going to run a level two EV charger that draws 7,200 W continuously or your central HVAC heat'

[16:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pump system. But that\'s not this product\'s job or intended [music] use case. One Pila unit is priced at $1,499. For a lot of stuff in the world right now, pricing is a moving target. Raw materials, supply chain, tariffs, all of it\'s in motion. I hope those factors'

[16:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'don\'t make this category too expensive to build a custom base, [music] but for now, Pila\'s price is locked in. And competitors are coming. I can almost guarantee you that there are companies in China already working on copycats. In a weird way, that\'s a good thing. It'

[16:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'means the category is real, but it also means the question of who you buy from really starts to matter because safety and software are the factors that will make the difference for consumers. So, who is this actually for? Well, it\'s'

[16:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for renters and apartment dwellers who\'ve never been able to own a home battery before. It\'s for homeowners like me who hit a code limit. It\'s for people who move, &gt;&gt; [laughter] &gt;&gt; which is most of us, and don\'t want to leave thousands of dollars of hardware'

[16:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'behind. And the big question for why you\'d want it? Well, that\'s going to vary based on, well, you. I live in your screen, not in your head. But the big ones are energy security and saving money. Pila can keep'

[17:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your gear running through a blackout, whether that\'s an office setup that you need running to save your work, or your fridge running to save your produce from spoiling, [music] or even your medicine. If you have time-of-use rates, these batteries can shift when you use your'

[17:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'energy and save you big money every single year. On the other hand, Pillo is not for anyone who wants a Powerwall replacement today. It\'s not for anyone who wants a single unit to run their whole house. So, here\'s my take. The category matters'

[17:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more than the specific product. What Pillo is doing is the first credible version of something that should have existed a decade ago. Home batteries shouldn\'t require an electrician, a permit, and a kitchen renovation budget. They should just plug in. And the power'

[17:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gap in my garage, that one that I thought I couldn\'t close, is filled with these three batteries across three different rooms. It\'s a solution that works better than I could have expected. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Now, again, you should buy a product for what it can do for you today, not for a'

[17:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'promise of something to come. But, why I\'m excited about this product and why I\'m involved with this company is because of what it might do in the near future. Imagine when the UL 3700 code gets passed and you can start to put'

[18:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'energy back into a standard outlet in your home. That means a mesh Pillo system will start to act like a mini virtual power plant right inside your house. Now, expand that to a mid-size apartment building. 100 units at Pillo scale is about 160 to 320 kWh of'

[18:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'distributed storage assembled from plug-in modules. The kind of system that would normally need a concrete pad, switch gear, and a year of permitting. Pillo gets it there in minutes. [music]'

